---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import { site } from '../../content';
const path = '/guides/deliverability';
const title = 'Shopify SMS deliverability: diagnose the path to delivery';
const desc = 'Separate permission exclusions, provider queues, carrier rejection and missing receipts before changing your Shopify SMS program.';
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
  <ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} dateModified="2026-11-25" />
  <article class="container py-16 prose-shopify">
    <a href="/guides">← All SMS guides</a>
    <h1>Shopify SMS deliverability: diagnose the path to delivery</h1>
    <p>A customer missing a text does not identify the failed step. Establish whether the message was eligible, released, accepted by the provider, passed to a carrier, or reported delivered. Changing apps before locating the break can move the same problem into another dashboard.</p>
    <h2>Keep permission separate from transport</h2>
    <p>A phone field, an order or email subscription does not establish promotional SMS permission. Start with the channel record and capture evidence. A correctly excluded recipient is a successful permission control, not a carrier failure. Use the <a href="/guides/consent">consent guide</a> to investigate evidence and the <a href="/guides/list-growth">list-growth guide</a> to trace capture sources. This page starts where those records become a send decision.</p>
    <p><a href="https://help.klaviyo.com/hc/en-us/articles/1260806260849">Klaviyo distinguishes recipients skipped for missing SMS consent from delivery failures</a>. Its documentation also warns that delivery reports can be delayed or absent. Treat dashboard labels as provider-specific evidence, rather than assuming every sender uses the same state model.</p>
    <h2>Build a message-level state ledger</h2>
    <table class="data-table"><thead><tr><th>Stage</th><th>What to inspect</th><th>Next action</th></tr></thead><tbody>
      <tr><td>Excluded before dispatch</td><td>Consent, suppression, audience membership and purchase exit at evaluation time</td><td>Resolve an incorrect input; preserve a legitimate exclusion</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Deferred by policy</td><td>Quiet-hours or frequency decision, next evaluation and latest useful send time</td><td>Wait within the approved policy or cancel an expired message</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Provider accepted or queued</td><td>Provider message identifier, queue entry and current status</td><td>Investigate queue age and sender readiness before submitting again</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Rejected before carrier handoff</td><td>Request error, sender configuration, account limits and destination support</td><td>Correct the documented cause and review eligibility before a new attempt</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Sent without a final receipt</td><td>Last update, receipt availability and provider definition of sent</td><td>Keep the outcome unresolved; do not label it delivered or automatically resend</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Reported undelivered</td><td>Raw error, mapped reason, destination and sender</td><td>Separate filtering from device or number problems; escalate ambiguous reasons</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Reported delivered</td><td>Receipt time and source</td><td>Record transport evidence separately from reading, clicks and orders</td></tr>
    </tbody></table>
    <p>For a concrete API example, <a href="https://www.twilio.com/docs/messaging/api/message-resource">Twilio documents accepted, queued, sent, failed, delivered and undelivered states</a>. Those labels should not be imposed on every Shopify app. Ask your supplier which visible status corresponds to each stage and whether its export preserves the original reason. Acceptance acknowledges a request; it does not prove final delivery.</p>
    <h2>Distinguish deferral from rejection</h2>
    <p>A message waiting for a permitted dispatch window needs a different remedy from an invalid sender or carrier-filtered message. Keep the policy decision, scheduled release, expiry and actual dispatch time together. A purchase or withdrawal of permission while it waits should trigger a fresh eligibility decision. The <a href="/guides/quiet-hours">quiet-hours guide</a> owns destination policy, unknown-zone fallback and daylight-saving checks; no universal hour window applies here.</p>
    <p>For a queue that outlives an offer, ask whether expiry applies only inside the provider or also downstream. Twilio documents a queue validity period; it is not a promise that a carrier cannot delay a message after handoff. Establish the cancellation boundary before scheduling stock-limited or deadline-dependent texts.</p>
    <h2>Investigate failures without guessing the cause</h2>
    <p>Group a change in failures by destination, sender, message version, capture source and time interval. Compare like traffic: a different market or a different mix of new subscribers can change the aggregate without proving a platform regression. Keep raw reasons alongside your categories so a later support explanation can revise the mapping.</p>
    <p>Klaviyo's <a href="https://help.klaviyo.com/hc/en-us/articles/360039239172">failure-reason documentation</a> separates device problems and carrier violations. A blocked label can involve different actors, so it is not enough to conclude that the carrier rejected the copy. Ask support to interpret the specific record. Do not rotate senders, disguise content or re-import suppressed numbers to bypass a rejection.</p>
    <h2>Prepare a useful support packet</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Campaign or flow identifier, provider message identifiers and relevant UTC timestamps.</li>
      <li>Destination market and sender type, with personal data shared only through the supplier's appropriate support channel.</li>
      <li>Exact approved message and linked destination version, including the offer's expiry.</li>
      <li>Raw status history, error codes, permission decision and the point where expected and actual behavior diverged.</li>
      <li>Scope of affected traffic and a comparable unaffected group, without claiming a cause from correlation.</li>
    </ul>
    <h2>Proposed acceptance rehearsal</h2>
    <p>Use controlled profiles and supported destinations. Exercise an email-only subscriber, a valid text subscriber, an opt-out while queued, a purchase before release and an offer expiring during deferral. Verify that each reaches the intended state. Inspect a documented rejection through the supplier's supported test mechanism or existing diagnostic records; do not provoke prohibited traffic to manufacture an error. These are proposed checks, not campaign results.</p>
    <p>Save eligibility and transport evidence separately. For reporting, define your denominator: receipt-confirmed deliveries divided by carrier-submitted attempts answers a different question from delivered messages divided by the original audience. Keep exclusions, pending outcomes and failures visible beside either ratio. A delivery receipt does not demonstrate incremental sales, and an unresolved receipt is not automatically a failure.</p>
    <h2>Use the diagnosis to choose the next investment</h2>
    <p>If the break is capture evidence, fix the form and sync path. If it is cancellation after purchase, inspect event latency and queue ownership. If it is an unexplained rejection, require supplier diagnostics for the actual destination and sender. Compare candidates through <a href="/apps">the app directory</a> using the same evidence packet and acceptance tasks. Buy a missing capability or service commitment, rather than a promised universal deliverability improvement.</p>
    <p>Retain cost and channel coordination in the decision: use <a href="/guides/pricing">the pricing worksheet</a> for billable attempts and support costs, and <a href="/guides/email-sms-coordination">email and SMS coordination</a> for overlapping journeys. Neither lower price nor another channel repairs a broken permission or transport record.</p>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
